{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BtKKhgbn97Dp8AqdY3FpWb","type":"tool_result","content":"354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6bc825b2-855a-40f4-bda9-b6466b178977","uuid":"49d5d6b6-2c52-419c-8828-b3a8cdcb6693","timestamp":"2026-05-17T09:20:59.363Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.","numLines":50,"startLine":354,"totalLines":969}}}
